    <!-- OUTSIDE PLUM'S LANE -->
    <h2 style="margin-top:36px;margin-bottom:8px;">What sits outside insurance — and who should own it</h2>
    <p class="section-intro">These aren't insurance products, so they're outside Plum's scope. But they're part of your total offer, and senior candidates will ask about them. Your CHRO, PEO, or India HR lead should have a view on each one before your first senior hire conversation.</p>

    <div class="outside-lane">
      <h3>The non-insurance layer</h3>
      <p>Some of these are statutory; some are market expectations. All of them come up in senior hire conversations sooner than most MNCs expect.</p>
      <div class="outside-grid">
        <div class="outside-item">
          <div class="outside-item-label">Leave policy</div>
          <div class="outside-item-value">Statutory: 12–15 earned leave days. Global MNC standard: 20–25 days + sick leave + public holidays. Unlimited leave is rare but growing in GCCs.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="outside-item">
          <div class="outside-item-label">ESOPs / equity</div>
          <div class="outside-item-value">Not a legal requirement, but effectively mandatory for senior GCC hires benchmarking against global comp. Indian ESOP taxation changed in 2021 — verify with a tax advisor.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="outside-item">
          <div class="outside-item-label">Learning &amp; development</div>
          <div class="outside-item-value">₹20,000–₹60,000 annual L&D budget per employee is common in IT and GCC sector. Global startups in India often extend access to global L&D platforms (Coursera, LinkedIn Learning).</div>
        </div>
        <div class="outside-item">
          <div class="outside-item-label">Hybrid / remote policy</div>
          <div class="outside-item-value">Not regulated, but a significant offer-letter factor in Bangalore, Mumbai, and Hyderabad tech markets. Most GCCs offer 2–3 WFH days. Full-remote is still rare but increasing for senior specialist roles.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="outside-item">
          <div class="outside-item-label">Food &amp; transport</div>
          <div class="outside-item-value">Meal allowance: ₹2,200/month is the tax-exempt limit. Transport allowance: ₹1,600/month tax-exempt. Both are standard in the Indian CTC structure — often included as allowances rather than cash reimbursements.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="outside-item">
          <div class="outside-item-label">Internet &amp; WFH stipend</div>
          <div class="outside-item-value">Post-pandemic standard in tech companies. ₹1,500–₹3,000/month for internet reimbursement. One-time WFH setup allowance (₹10,000–₹25,000) offered by global startups entering India.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="callout callout-teal" style="margin-top:20px;">
      <span class="callout-icon">ℹ</span>
      <div><strong>Run this calculation before your next renewal:</strong> on a ₹20.2 crore total compensation budget, moving from 1% to 2% on benefits costs 1 percentage point on salary increments — a 12% hike becomes 11%. For that difference, you go from ₹5L sum insured with no parental cover to ₹7–10L, parental cover, progressive maternity, and primary healthcare. 70% of employees say they'd take the better benefits over the better hike. Most HR teams haven't shown this maths to their CFO.</div>
    </div>
  </section>

